          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lawrence-women-in-love-00ed39a80fe9339f\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Gerald’s why-question presupposes or provisionally concedes that he does not love Gudrun and asks for the reason. Its core force is therefore truth-oriented: it presents the absence of a loving state as true or admitted. It neither undertakes a relational commitment nor functions as a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The interrogative form literally requests an explanation, but the narrator explicitly frames it as an admission, strongly supporting T rather than a merely information-seeking reading.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Gerald directly asks Gudrun, “Why don’t I love you?” during their confrontation.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Gudrun has coldly claimed that Gerald loves her “very little indeed.” His response effectively accepts her accusation while expressing hostility toward her for compelling that admission. The subsequent exchange confirms this interpretation: he repeatedly denies having loved her or being able to love her. His anger and emotional distress characterize the delivery and conflict but do not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Why don’t I love you?” he asked, as if admitting the truth of her accusation, yet hating her for it.\",\"supports\":\"The narrator explicitly construes the question as an admission that Gerald does not love Gudrun, strongly supporting truth-conditional force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Gudrun answers, “I don’t know why you don’t,” treating Gerald’s lack of love as shared conversational ground.\",\"supports\":\"The immediate response confirms the presuppositional reading of the question rather than treating it as a declaration or undertaking of love.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"When asked whether he can love her, has ever loved her, or will love her, Gerald repeatedly answers “No.”\",\"supports\":\"The ensuing dialogue corroborates that the highlighted utterance presents an absence of loving feeling, not a commitment or expressive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as a present-tense negative interrogative in Chapter XXX of Women in Love.\",\"supports\":\"The grammatical form supports analysis as a why-question concerning a negatively presented emotional state.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the utterance’s presuppositional admission concerning Gerald’s lack of love. The interrogative request for a cause and the surrounding antagonism require contextual explanation but do not expose a missing core-force category.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The negative polarity changes the represented state from love to absence of love, but not the truth-oriented mode of presentation.\"}"
        }
      ],
